('23', '3', '1', '2', '\\documentclass[landscape]{book}\r\n\r\n\\title{Landscape Layout}\r\n\\author{Physics Content}\r\n\\date{\\today}\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n	\r\n	\\maketitle\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Fluid Mechanics}\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Introduction}\r\n	\r\n	Fluid mechanics studies the behavior of fluids (liquids and gases) and the forces on them. \r\n	\r\n	\\section{Buoyancy}\r\n	\r\n	The buoyant force is an upward force exerted by a fluid on an immersed object.\r\n	\r\n\\end{document}', NULL, NULL),
('24', '3', '1', '3', '\\documentclass[oneside]{book}\r\n\r\n\\title{One-Sided Layout}\r\n\\author{Physics Content}\r\n\\date{\\today}\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n	\r\n	\\maketitle\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Special Relativity}\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Introduction}\r\n	\r\n	Special relativity deals with objects moving at constant speeds close to the speed of light.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Time Dilation}\r\n	\r\n	Time dilates or stretches out as objects move faster, especially as they approach the speed of light.\r\n	\r\n\\end{document}', NULL, NULL),
('25', '3', '2', '1', '\\documentclass{book}\r\n\r\n\\title{Basic Chapter, Section, and Subsection}\r\n\\author{Physics Content}\r\n\\date{\\today}\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n\r\n\\maketitle\r\n\r\n\\chapter{Introduction to Physics}\r\n\r\n\\section{Classical Mechanics}\r\n\r\n\\subsection{Kinematics}\r\n\r\n\\subsubsection{Displacement}\r\n\r\n\\paragraph{Definition}\r\n\r\n\\subparagraph{Details}\r\n\r\n\\section{Electromagnetism}\r\n\r\n\\subsection{Electric Fields}\r\n\r\n\\subsubsection{Coulomb\'s Law}\r\n\r\n\\end{document}', NULL, NULL),
('26', '3', '2', '2', '\\documentclass{article}\r\n\r\n\\title{Adding Sections with Unnumbered Titles}\r\n\\author{Physics Content}\r\n\\date{\\today}\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n\r\n\\maketitle\r\n\r\n\\section*{Introduction}\r\n\r\n\\subsection*{Background}\r\n\r\n\\subsubsection*{History}\r\n\r\n\\section*{Conclusion}\r\n\r\n\\end{document}', NULL, NULL),
('27', '3', '2', '3', '\\documentclass{report}\r\n\r\n\\usepackage{titlesec}\r\n\r\n\\title{Custom Sectioning with Titles}\r\n\\author{Physics Content}\r\n\\date{\\today}\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n\r\n\\maketitle\r\n\r\n\\titleformat{\\chapter}[block]{\\bfseries\\Large}{Chapter \\thechapter:}{1em}{}\r\n\\titleformat{\\section}[block]{\\bfseries\\large}{\\thesection}{1em}{}\r\n\\titleformat{\\subsection}[block]{\\bfseries}{\\thesubsection}{1em}{}\r\n\r\n\\chapter{Overview}\r\n\r\nThis chapter provides a comprehensive overview of the fundamental principles in physics, setting the stage for more detailed discussions.\r\n\r\n\\section{Main Topics}\r\n\r\nWe will explore several key topics, including classical mechanics, electromagnetism, and quantum theory.\r\n\r\n\\subsection{Detailed Analysis}\r\n\r\nA detailed analysis involves breaking down complex phenomena into simpler components, allowing for a deeper understanding of underlying mechanisms.\r\n\r\n\\chapter{Applications}\r\n\r\nThis chapter covers the practical applications of theoretical concepts in various fields, including technology and engineering.\r\n\r\n\\section{Technological Innovations}\r\n\r\nRecent technological advancements are often rooted in fundamental physics principles, such as semiconductors and lasers.\r\n\r\n\\subsection{Engineering Solutions}\r\n\r\nPhysics plays a critical role in designing and optimizing engineering solutions, from structural integrity to energy efficiency.\r\n\r\n\\end{document}', NULL, NULL),
('28', '3', '2', '4', '\\documentclass{book}\r\n\r\n\\title{Using \\textbackslash part for Large Divisions}\r\n\\author{Physics Content}\r\n\\date{\\today}\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n	\r\n	\\maketitle\r\n	\r\n	\\part{Fundamentals of Physics}\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Mechanics}\r\n	\r\n	Mechanics is the branch of physics dealing with the motion of objects and the forces acting on them. It includes concepts like force, mass, and acceleration.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Newton\'s Laws}\r\n	\r\n	Newton\'s Laws describe the relationship between the motion of an object and the forces acting on it. They form the foundation of classical mechanics.\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Thermodynamics}\r\n	\r\n	Thermodynamics explores the principles governing heat, energy, and work. It includes laws such as the conservation of energy and entropy.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Laws of Thermodynamics}\r\n	\r\n	The laws of thermodynamics provide a framework for understanding energy transfer and transformation in physical systems.\r\n	\r\n	\\part{Advanced Topics}\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Quantum Mechanics}\r\n	\r\n	Quantum mechanics explores the behavior of particles at the atomic and subatomic levels, where classical physics does not apply.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Uncertainty Principle}\r\n	\r\n	The uncertainty principle, formulated by Heisenberg, states that certain pairs of physical properties cannot be simultaneously known to arbitrary precision.\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Relativity}\r\n	\r\n	Relativity revolutionizes our understanding of space and time, introducing concepts like time dilation and length contraction.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Special Relativity}\r\n	\r\n	Special relativity addresses the behavior of objects moving at high speeds and introduces the famous equation \\(E = mc^2\\).\r\n	\r\n\\end{document}', NULL, NULL),
('29', '3', '2', '5', '\\documentclass{article}\r\n\r\n\\title{Customizing Section Numbering}\r\n\\author{Physics Content}\r\n\\date{\\today}\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n	\r\n	\\maketitle\r\n	\r\n	\\setcounter{section}{5}\r\n	\\setcounter{subsection}{3}\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Modified Sections}\r\n	\r\n	This section starts with number 6 due to previous modifications. It discusses advanced topics in physics.\r\n	\r\n	\\subsection{Subsection Starting at 4}\r\n	\r\n	This subsection continues the numbering from the modified section count, addressing specific details in the analysis.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Additional Content}\r\n	\r\n	\\section{New Topics}\r\n	\r\n	This chapter introduces new topics and areas of interest that were not covered in earlier sections.\r\n	\r\n\\end{document}', NULL, NULL),
('30', '3', '2', '6', '\\documentclass{report}\r\n\r\n\\title{Adding a Table of Contents}\r\n\\author{Physics Content}\r\n\\date{\\today}\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n	\r\n	\\maketitle\r\n	\r\n	\\tableofcontents\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Introduction}\r\n	\r\n	The introduction provides an overview of the physics topics covered in this report, including classical mechanics and electromagnetism.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Background}\r\n	\r\n	This section covers the historical development of key physics concepts and theories.\r\n	\r\n	\\subsection{Historical Context}\r\n	\r\n	A detailed look at the milestones in physics, from ancient Greece to modern scientific advancements.\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Theory}\r\n	\r\n	The theory chapter delves into fundamental principles and mathematical frameworks used in physics.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Fundamentals}\r\n	\r\n	Basic principles of physics such as Newton\'s Laws, conservation laws, and fundamental interactions are discussed here.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Applications}\r\n	\r\n	Application of fundamental theories to solve real-world problems and technological advancements.\r\n	\r\n\\end{document}', NULL, NULL),
('31', '3', '2', '7', '\\documentclass{book}\r\n\r\n\\usepackage{fancyhdr}\r\n\r\n\\title{Using \\textbackslash chaptermark and \\textbackslash sectionmark for Custom Headers}\r\n\\author{Physics Content}\r\n\\date{\\today}\r\n\r\n\\pagestyle{fancy}\r\n\\fancyhead[L]{\\leftmark}\r\n\\fancyhead[R]{\\rightmark}\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n	\r\n	\\maketitle\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Introduction}\r\n	\\sectionmark{Basic Concepts}\r\n	\r\n	This chapter introduces the main topics and scope of the physics content covered in this book. Physics is a vast field that encompasses many fundamental concepts and theories. Understanding these concepts is crucial for grasping the more advanced topics that will be discussed later in the book. We will start by exploring the basics and gradually move towards more complex ideas, ensuring that each concept builds upon the previous ones.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Basic Concepts}\r\n	\r\n	An overview of fundamental concepts in physics, including force, energy, and motion. These concepts form the foundation of classical mechanics, a branch of physics that deals with the motion of objects and the forces acting upon them. Force is a vector quantity that represents an interaction that changes the motion of an object. Energy, on the other hand, is a scalar quantity that represents the capacity to do work. Motion describes the change in position of an object over time.\r\n	\r\n	The study of these basic concepts leads to a deeper understanding of various physical phenomena, such as the laws of motion formulated by Isaac Newton. Newton\'s laws are essential in explaining how objects move and interact with forces. For instance, Newton\'s First Law states that an object will remain at rest or in uniform motion unless acted upon by an external force. This law forms the basis for understanding inertia and the concept of equilibrium.\r\n	\r\n	To further illustrate these concepts, consider a simple example of a car accelerating on a highway. The force exerted by the engine propels the car forward, changing its velocity and thus its motion. The car\'s energy changes as it accelerates, converting chemical energy from the fuel into kinetic energy of motion. Understanding these interactions helps in analyzing and predicting the behavior of objects in various scenarios.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Advanced Topics}\r\n	\r\n	Exploration of more complex topics in physics such as quantum mechanics and general relativity. Quantum mechanics is a branch of physics that deals with phenomena at very small scales, such as atoms and subatomic particles. It introduces concepts like wave-particle duality and quantization, which challenge classical intuitions about the nature of matter and energy.\r\n	\r\n	Quantum mechanics has profound implications for our understanding of the universe. For example, the concept of superposition allows particles to exist in multiple states simultaneously until measured. This principle leads to the phenomenon of quantum entanglement, where particles become correlated in such a way that the state of one particle instantaneously influences the state of another, regardless of the distance between them.\r\n	\r\n	On the other hand, general relativity is a theory of gravitation proposed by Albert Einstein. It describes gravity not as a force, but as the curvature of spacetime caused by the presence of mass and energy. This theory has been confirmed through various experiments and observations, such as the bending of light around massive objects and the precise measurements of gravitational waves. General relativity provides a more accurate description of gravitational interactions, especially in strong gravitational fields and at cosmic scales.\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Conclusion}\r\n	\r\n	Summary of the key findings and implications of the discussed topics. In this book, we have covered a range of topics from fundamental concepts to advanced theories in physics. By understanding these concepts, we gain insight into the underlying principles that govern the natural world.\r\n	\r\n	The exploration of basic concepts such as force, energy, and motion provides a solid foundation for delving into more complex topics like quantum mechanics and general relativity. These advanced theories offer new perspectives on the behavior of matter and energy, challenging our perceptions and expanding our knowledge of the universe.\r\n	\r\n	In conclusion, the study of physics is a journey that reveals the intricacies of the natural world. It fosters critical thinking and problem-solving skills, which are valuable in various scientific and technological fields. As we continue to explore and uncover the mysteries of the universe, our understanding of physics will continue to evolve, driving further advancements and discoveries.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Summary}\r\n	\r\n	A recap of the main points and conclusions drawn from the content covered in previous chapters. This book has provided a comprehensive overview of key concepts and theories in physics. By exploring both fundamental and advanced topics, we have gained a deeper appreciation for the complexity and beauty of the physical world.\r\n	\r\n	Understanding the basic concepts of force, energy, and motion sets the stage for studying more intricate topics like quantum mechanics and general relativity. These theories not only enhance our understanding of the universe but also inspire further research and exploration. The journey through physics is one of discovery and learning, with each new insight leading to a greater understanding of the natural world.\r\n	\r\n\\end{document}', NULL, NULL),
('32', '3', '2', '8', '\\documentclass{book}\r\n\r\n\\title{Using \\textbackslash part and \\textbackslash chapter for Comprehensive Division}\r\n\\author{Physics Content}\r\n\\date{\\today}\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n	\r\n	\\maketitle\r\n	\r\n	\\part{Introduction to Physics}\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Fundamentals}\r\n	\r\n	This part covers the basic principles and concepts fundamental to the study of physics.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Basic Concepts}\r\n	\r\n	Overview of fundamental concepts such as force, energy, and motion.\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Classical Mechanics}\r\n	\r\n	Detailed discussion on classical mechanics, including kinematics and dynamics.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Kinematics}\r\n	\r\n	The study of motion without considering the forces that cause it.\r\n	\r\n	\\part{Advanced Topics}\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Quantum Mechanics}\r\n	\r\n	Exploration of quantum mechanics and its principles.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Wave-Particle Duality}\r\n	\r\n	The concept that particles exhibit both wave-like and particle-like properties.\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Relativity}\r\n	\r\n	An introduction to the theory of relativity and its implications.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{General Relativity}\r\n	\r\n	The theory of gravitation formulated by Albert Einstein, describing gravity as a curvature of spacetime.\r\n	\r\n\\end{document}', NULL, NULL),
('33', '3', '2', '9', '\\documentclass{book}\r\n\r\n\\title{Detailed Chapter and Section Content}\r\n\\author{Physics Content}\r\n\\date{\\today}\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n	\r\n	\\maketitle\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Electromagnetism}\r\n	\r\n	Electromagnetism is the study of electric and magnetic fields and their interactions. It forms the basis of many modern technologies.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Electric Fields}\r\n	\r\n	An electric field is a field around a charged particle where other charges experience a force. \r\n	\r\n	\\subsection{Coulomb\'s Law}\r\n	\r\n	Coulomb\'s Law describes the force between two charged particles. It is essential for understanding electrostatic interactions.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Magnetic Fields}\r\n	\r\n	Magnetic fields are produced by moving charges and are described by various laws and equations.\r\n	\r\n	\\subsection{Lorentz Force}\r\n	\r\n	The Lorentz Force Law states that a charged particle moving in a magnetic field experiences a force perpendicular to both its velocity and the magnetic field.\r\n	\r\n	\\chapter{Quantum Mechanics}\r\n	\r\n	Quantum mechanics deals with the behavior of particles at the atomic and subatomic levels, where classical mechanics fails.\r\n	\r\n	\\section{Wave-Particle Duality}\r\n	\r\n	Wave-particle duality is the concept that every particle exhibits both wave and particle properties.\r\n	\r\n	\\subsection{Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle}\r\n	\r\n	The Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle asserts that certain pairs of physical properties cannot be simultaneously measured with arbitrary precision.\r\n	\r\n\\end{document}', NULL, NULL),
('34', '6', '2', '1', '\\documentclass{article}\r\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n\r\nThe quadratic function is given by:\r\n\r\n\\[\r\nf(x) = ax^2 + bx + c\r\n\\]\r\n\r\nwhere \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) are constants.\r\n\r\n\\end{document}', NULL, NULL),
('35', '6', '2', '2', '\\documentclass{article}\r\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\r\n\r\n\\title{Exponential Function}\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n\r\n\\maketitle\r\n\r\nThe exponential function is expressed as:\r\n\r\n\\[\r\nf(x) = e^x\r\n\\]\r\n\r\nwhere \\(e\\) is the base of the natural logarithm.\r\n\r\n\\end{document}', NULL, NULL),
('36', '6', '2', '3', '\\documentclass{article}\r\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\r\n\r\n\\title{Logarithmic Function}\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n\r\n\\maketitle\r\n\r\nThe logarithmic function is given by:\r\n\r\n\\[\r\nf(x) = \\log_b(x)\r\n\\]\r\n\r\nwhere \\(b\\) is the base of the logarithm.\r\n\r\n\\end{document}', NULL, NULL),
('37', '6', '2', '4', '\\documentclass{article}\r\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\r\n\r\n\\title{Gaussian Function}\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n\r\n\\maketitle\r\n\r\nThe Gaussian function is:\r\n\r\n\\[\r\nf(x) = A e^{-\\frac{(x - \\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}}\r\n\\]\r\n\r\nwhere \\(A\\) is the amplitude, \\(\\mu\\) is the mean, and \\(\\sigma\\) is the standard deviation.\r\n\r\n\\end{document}', NULL, NULL),
