('104', '1', '9', '3', '# Writing structured data to a file\r\ndata = [[\"ID\", \"Name\", \"Score\"], [1, \"ABC\", 85], [2, \"DEF\", 90], [3, \"GHI\", 78]]\r\nwith open(\'data.txt\', \'w\') as file:\r\n    for row in data:\r\n        file.write(\" | \".join(map(str, row)) + \"\\n\")\r\n\r\n# Reading structured data from the file\r\nwith open(\'data.txt\', \'r\') as file:\r\n    content = file.read()\r\n    print(content)', NULL, ''),
('105', '6', '2', '1', 'import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\r\n\r\n# Initial conditions\r\nx = 1.0  # initial position\r\nv = 0.0  # initial velocity\r\nt = 0.0  # initial time\r\n\r\n# Time parameters\r\ndt = 0.1  # time step\r\nsteps = 1000  # number of steps\r\n\r\ndef F(x, t, v):\r\n    # Example: Harmonic oscillator force -kx\r\n    k = 1.0  # spring constant\r\n    return -k*x \r\n\r\nX, V, T = [x], [v], [t]\r\nfor i in range(steps):\r\n    x = x + v * dt \r\n    v = v + F(x, t, v) * dt\r\n    \r\n    t = t + dt\r\n    X.append(x)\r\n    V.append(v)\r\n    T.append(t)\r\n\r\n# Plotting\r\nplt.figure(figsize=(10,20))\r\n\r\nplt.subplot(311)\r\nplt.plot(T, X)\r\nplt.title(\'Position vs Time\')\r\nplt.xlabel(\'Time\')\r\nplt.ylabel(\'Position\')\r\n\r\nplt.subplot(312)\r\nplt.plot(X, V)\r\nplt.title(\'Position vs Velocity\')\r\nplt.xlabel(\'Position\')\r\nplt.ylabel(\'Velocity\')\r\n\r\nplt.subplot(313)\r\nplt.plot(T, V)\r\nplt.title(\'Time vs Velocity\')\r\nplt.xlabel(\'Time\')\r\nplt.ylabel(\'Velocity\')\r\n\r\nplt.tight_layout()\r\nplt.savefig(\"plot.png\")\r\nplt.show()', '        <script id=\"MathJax-script\" async src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@3/es5/tex-mml-chtml.js\"></script>\r\n\r\n    \r\n    <p>This Python program simulates the motion of a one-dimensional harmonic oscillator using numerical integration (Euler\'s method). The harmonic oscillator follows Hooke\'s Law, where the force acting on the object is proportional to its displacement and is given by:</p>\r\n    \r\n    <p>\r\n        \\[\r\n        F = -kx\r\n        \\]\r\n    </p>\r\n    \r\n    <p>Where:</p>\r\n    <ul>\r\n        <li>\\( F \\) is the force acting on the object</li>\r\n        <li>\\( k \\) is the spring constant</li>\r\n        <li>\\( x \\) is the displacement of the object from the equilibrium position</li>\r\n    </ul>', ''),
('106', '6', '1', '1', 'import numpy as np\r\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\r\n\r\n# Define the ODE as a function\r\ndef f(x, y):\r\n    return x + y\r\n\r\n# Euler\'s method implementation using a for loop\r\ndef euler_method(f, x0, y0, h, x_end):\r\n    # Initialize the lists to store x and y values\r\n    x_values = [x0]\r\n    y_values = [y0]\r\n    \r\n    # Determine the number of steps\r\n    n_steps = int((x_end - x0) / h)\r\n    \r\n    # Iteratively apply Euler\'s method using a for loop\r\n    for _ in range(n_steps):\r\n        # Calculate the next y value\r\n        y_new = y_values[-1] + h * f(x_values[-1], y_values[-1])\r\n        \r\n        # Calculate the next x value\r\n        x_new = x_values[-1] + h\r\n        \r\n        # Append the new values to the lists\r\n        x_values.append(x_new)\r\n        y_values.append(y_new)\r\n    \r\n    return x_values, y_values\r\n\r\n# Parameters\r\nx0 = 0          # Initial x value\r\ny0 = 1          # Initial y value\r\nh = 0.1         # Step size\r\nx_end = 2       # End value of x\r\n\r\n# Solve the ODE using Euler\'s method\r\nx_values, y_values = euler_method(f, x0, y0, h, x_end)\r\n\r\n# Print the results\r\nprint(f\"x values: {x_values}, y values: {y_values}\")\r\n\r\n# Plot the results\r\nplt.plot(x_values, y_values, \'b-\', label=\"Euler\'s Method Approximation\")\r\nplt.xlabel(\'x\')\r\nplt.ylabel(\'y\')\r\nplt.title(\"Solving ODE using Euler\'s Method\")\r\nplt.legend()\r\nplt.grid(True)\r\nplt.savefig(\'plot.png\')\r\nplt.show()', '\r\n        <script id=\"MathJax-script\" async src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@3/es5/tex-mml-chtml.js\"></script>\r\n\r\n    <h1>Euler\'s Method for Solving ODE</h1>\r\n\r\n    <p>To solve the first-order ODE given by:</p>\r\n    <p>\\(\\frac{dy}{dx} = f(x, y)\\)</p>\r\n    <p>with an initial value:</p>\r\n    <p>\\(y(x_0) = y_0\\)</p>\r\n\r\n    <p>Euler\'s method uses the following formula to approximate the value of \\(y\\) at each step:</p>\r\n    <p>\\(y_{i+1} = y_i + h \\cdot f(x_i, y_i)\\)</p>\r\n\r\n    <h3>Where:</h3>\r\n    <ul>\r\n        <li>\\(h\\) is the step size (how far you move along the x-axis at each step).</li>\r\n        <li>\\(y_i\\) is the current value of \\(y\\) at \\(x_i\\).</li>\r\n        <li>\\(f(x_i, y_i)\\) is the slope at the point \\((x_i, y_i)\\).</li>\r\n    </ul>\r\n\r\n    <h2>Steps to Implement:</h2>\r\n    <ol>\r\n        <li>Write down the ODE and the initial value.</li>\r\n        <li>Choose a step size \\(h\\). Smaller steps usually give better results.</li>\r\n        <li>Start with the initial value \\(y_0\\).</li>\r\n        <li>Use the formula to calculate \\(y\\) at each step \\(x_i\\).</li>\r\n    </ol>\r\n\r\n\r\n    <div class=\"algorithm\">\r\n        <h3>Algorithm: Euler\'s Method for Solving ODE</h3>\r\n\r\n        <h3>Input:</h3>\r\n        <ul>\r\n            <li>Function \\(f(x, y)\\) representing the ODE \\(\\frac{dy}{dx} = f(x, y)\\).</li>\r\n            <li>Initial condition \\(x_0\\), \\(y_0\\) where \\(y(x_0) = y_0\\).</li>\r\n            <li>Step size \\(h\\).</li>\r\n            <li>End value \\(x_{\\text{end}}\\) where the solution is to be approximated.</li>\r\n        </ul>\r\n\r\n        <h3>Output:</h3>\r\n        <p>Approximate values of \\(y\\) at discrete points \\(x_0, x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n\\) where \\(x_n \\leq x_{\\text{end}}\\).</p>\r\n\r\n        <h3>Steps:</h3>\r\n        <ol>\r\n            <li><strong>Initialization:</strong>\r\n                <ul>\r\n                    <li>Set \\(x = x_0\\).</li>\r\n                    <li>Set \\(y = y_0\\).</li>\r\n                    <li>Initialize a list \\(x_values\\) to store the values of \\(x\\).</li>\r\n                    <li>Initialize a list \\(y_values\\) to store the values of \\(y\\).</li>\r\n                    <li>Add the initial values \\(x_0\\) and \\(y_0\\) to \\(x_values\\) and \\(y_values\\).</li>\r\n                </ul>\r\n            </li>\r\n            <li><strong>Iterate:</strong>\r\n                <ul>\r\n                    <li>While \\(x < x_{\\text{end}}\\):</li>\r\n                    <ul>\r\n                        <li>Calculate the slope at the current point using the function \\(f(x, y)\\): <br>\\(\\text{slope} = f(x, y)\\)</li>\r\n                        <li>Update the value of \\(y\\) using Euler\'s formula: <br>\\(y_{\\text{new}} = y + h \\cdot \\text{slope}\\)</li>\r\n                        <li>Update the value of \\(x\\) to the next step: <br>\\(x_{\\text{new}} = x + h\\)</li>\r\n                        <li>Append the new values \\(x_{\\text{new}}\\) and \\(y_{\\text{new}}\\) to \\(x_values\\) and \\(y_values\\).</li>\r\n                        <li>Set \\(x = x_{\\text{new}}\\) and \\(y = y_{\\text{new}}\\).</li>\r\n                    </ul>\r\n                </ul>\r\n            </li>\r\n            <li><strong>Termination:</strong>\r\n                <ul>\r\n                    <li>Continue the iteration until \\(x\\) reaches or exceeds \\(x_{\\text{end}}\\).</li>\r\n                </ul>\r\n            </li>\r\n            <li><strong>Output the Results:</strong>\r\n                <ul>\r\n                    <li>Return the lists \\(x_(values)\\) and \\(y_(values)\\), which contain the discrete points \\(x\\) and the corresponding approximated values \\(y\\).</li>\r\n                </ul>\r\n            </li>\r\n        </ol>\r\n    </div>\r\n\r\n    <div class=\"example\">\r\n        <h2>Example:</h2>\r\n        <p>Given the ODE:</p>\r\n        <p>\\(\\frac{dy}{dx} = x + y\\)</p>\r\n        <p>with the initial condition:</p>\r\n        <p>\\(y(0) = 1\\)</p>\r\n        <p>Letâ€™s find the value of \\(y\\) at \\(x = 0.2\\) with \\(h = 0.1\\).</p>\r\n    </div>\r\n', '');
INSERT INTO `python` (`id`, `menu_id`, `submenu_id`, `program_id`, `content`, `algo`, `explanation`) VALUES
('107', '6', '3', '1', 'import numpy as np\r\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\r\n\r\n# Parameters\r\nR = 5e3  # Resistance in ohms\r\nC = 1e-6  # Capacitance in farads\r\nV_in = 5  # Input voltage in volts\r\nt_max = 0.05  # Time duration for simulation in seconds\r\ndt = 1e-4  # Time step for Euler method in seconds\r\n\r\n# Number of steps\r\nn_steps = int(t_max / dt)\r\n\r\n# Arrays to store time and voltage\r\ntime = np.linspace(0, t_max, n_steps)\r\nvoltage_charging = np.zeros(n_steps)\r\nvoltage_discharging = np.zeros(n_steps)\r\n\r\n# Initial conditions\r\nvoltage_charging[0] = 0  # Initial voltage across capacitor for charging\r\nvoltage_discharging[0] = V_in  # Initial voltage across capacitor for discharging\r\n\r\n# Euler method for charging\r\nfor i in range(1, n_steps):\r\n    dV = (V_in - voltage_charging[i-1]) / (R * C)\r\n    voltage_charging[i] = voltage_charging[i-1] + dV * dt\r\n\r\n# Euler method for discharging\r\nfor i in range(1, n_steps):\r\n    dV = -voltage_discharging[i-1] / (R * C)\r\n    voltage_discharging[i] = voltage_discharging[i-1] + dV * dt\r\n\r\n# Plotting the results\r\nplt.plot(time, voltage_charging, label=\'Charging\', color=\'blue\')\r\nplt.plot(time, voltage_discharging, label=\'Discharging\', color=\'red\')\r\n\r\nplt.title(\'Capacitor Charging and Discharging\')\r\nplt.xlabel(\'Time (s)\')\r\nplt.ylabel(\'Voltage (V)\')\r\nplt.legend()\r\nplt.grid(True)\r\nplt.savefig(\'plot.png\')\r\nplt.show()', '\r\n\r\n\r\n    <h1>ODE for Capacitor Charging and Discharging</h1>\r\n    \r\n    <p>The voltage across a capacitor <em>V(t)</em> in an RC circuit can be described by the following ODE:</p>\r\n\r\n    <p class=\"equation\">Charging: <br> <code>dV(t)/dt = (1/RC) * (V<sub>in</sub> - V(t))</code></p>\r\n    \r\n    <p class=\"equation\">Discharging: <br> <code>dV(t)/dt = -V(t)/(RC)</code></p>\r\n\r\n    <p>Here:</p>\r\n    <ul>\r\n        <li><em>V(t)</em> is the voltage across the capacitor at time <em>t</em>.</li>\r\n        <li><em>V<sub>in</sub></em> is the input voltage (for charging).</li>\r\n        <li><em>R</em> is the resistance in ohms.</li>\r\n        <li><em>C</em> is the capacitance in farads.</li>\r\n    </ul>\r\n\r\n', ''),
('108', '6', '4', '1', 'import numpy as np\r\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\r\n\r\n# Parameters\r\nR = 5000  # Resistance in ohms\r\nC = 5e-6  # Capacitance in farads\r\nf = 50    # Frequency of AC supply in Hz\r\nV_peak = 10  # Peak value of AC voltage in volts\r\nt_max = 0.2 # Time duration for simulation in seconds\r\ndt = 1e-5    # Time step for Euler method in seconds\r\n\r\n# Number of steps\r\nn_steps = int(t_max / dt)\r\n\r\n# Time array\r\ntime = np.linspace(0, t_max, n_steps)\r\n\r\n# Rectified input voltage\r\nV_in = V_peak * np.abs(np.sin(2 * np.pi * f * time))\r\n\r\n# Voltage across capacitor\r\nV_cap = np.zeros(n_steps)\r\n\r\n# Euler method for solving the ODE\r\nfor i in range(1, n_steps):\r\n    if V_in[i] > V_cap[i-1]:  # Charging phase\r\n        dV = (V_in[i] - V_cap[i-1]) / (R * C)\r\n    else:  # Discharging phase\r\n        dV = -V_cap[i-1] / (R * C)\r\n    V_cap[i] = V_cap[i-1] + dV * dt\r\n\r\n# Plotting the results\r\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\r\n\r\nplt.plot(time, V_in, label=\'Rectified Input Voltage (V_in)\', color=\'orange\')\r\nplt.plot(time, V_cap, label=\'Capacitor Voltage (V_cap)\', color=\'blue\')\r\n\r\nplt.title(\'Half-Wave Rectifier with Capacitor Filter\')\r\nplt.xlabel(\'Time (s)\')\r\nplt.ylabel(\'Voltage (V)\')\r\nplt.legend()\r\nplt.grid(True)\r\nplt.savefig(\'plot.png\')\r\nplt.show()', '    <style>\r\n        body {\r\n            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\r\n            line-height: 1.6;\r\n            margin: 0px;\r\n        }\r\n        h1, h2 {\r\n            color: #333;\r\n        }\r\n        h2 {\r\n            margin-top: 20px;\r\n        }\r\n        p, ul {\r\n            margin: 10px 0;\r\n        }\r\n        .equation {\r\n            font-weight: bold;\r\n            font-size: 1.2em;\r\n        }\r\n        .code {\r\n            background-color: #f4f4f4;\r\n            border: 1px solid #ddd;\r\n            padding: 10px;\r\n            font-family: monospace;\r\n            white-space: pre-wrap;\r\n            overflow-x: auto;\r\n        }\r\n    </style>\r\n\r\n\r\n    <h1>Theory of Capacitor in Half-Wave Rectifier Circuit</h1>\r\n    \r\n    <h2>1. Half-Wave Rectifier with Capacitor Filter</h2>\r\n    <p>A half-wave rectifier converts an alternating current (AC) signal into a pulsating direct current (DC) signal by allowing only the positive half-cycles of the AC signal to pass through while blocking the negative half-cycles.</p>\r\n    <p>To smooth out the pulsating DC signal, a capacitor is placed across the load. The capacitor charges when the rectified voltage is higher than the voltage across it and discharges when the rectified voltage drops. This creates a more stable DC output.</p>\r\n\r\n    <h2>2. Mathematical Model</h2>\r\n    <p>The voltage <em>V(t)</em> across the capacitor is governed by different ordinary differential equations (ODEs) during the charging and discharging phases:</p>\r\n    \r\n    <p class=\"equation\">Charging Phase:</p>\r\n    <p class=\"code\">\r\n        dV(t)/dt = (1/RC) * (V_in(t) - V(t))\r\n    </p>\r\n    <p>This equation describes how the capacitor charges when the rectified input voltage <em>V_in(t)</em> is greater than the current capacitor voltage <em>V(t)</em>.</p>\r\n    \r\n    <p class=\"equation\">Discharging Phase:</p>\r\n    <p class=\"code\">\r\n        dV(t)/dt = -V(t)/(RC)\r\n    </p>\r\n    <p>This equation describes the discharging process when <em>V_in(t)</em> is less than <em>V(t)</em>, causing the capacitor to release its stored energy to maintain the load voltage.</p>', ''),
('109', '2', '1', '1', '\'\'\'list of numbers\'\'\'\r\nA = [1,4,5,2,3,2.2,4.3,6,9.1]\r\nprint(\'List A=\',A)\r\n\r\n\'\'\'list of names\'\'\'\r\nB = [\'Physics\', \'Chemistry\', \'Mathematics\', \'Zoology\', \'Computer Sc.\']\r\nprint(\'List B=\',B)\r\n\r\n\'\'\'Mixed List\'\'\'\r\nC = [\'python\', 4, 5, [5, \'abc\'], \'@#$\']\r\nprint(\'List C=\',C)', NULL, ''),
